WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.500
You've got to hand it to "The Mandalorian."&nbsp;
This possible plot hole has fans talking.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:09.420
"The Mandalorian" Season 3 finale is here, and&nbsp;
boy, did the showrunners prove once again that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:13.140
they know how to end a season. "The Return"&nbsp;
has all that any "Star Wars" fan could want:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:16.140
Mandalorians doing aerial&nbsp;
combat with their jetpacks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:20.160
Din Djarin going full "John Wick," and&nbsp;
Grogu finally putting his Force training&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:23.520
to work. However, there's one casualty in&nbsp;
the episode that didn't make much sense.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:26.700
The Darksaber has been a contentious&nbsp;
object throughout "The Mandalorian."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:30.720
Whoever wielded the ancient weapon had the&nbsp;
right to rule Mandalore, so Moff Gideon and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:34.380
Bo-Katan spent much of their time trying to&nbsp;
win it back. However, in the season finale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:38.940
Gideon does a complete 180, destroying the&nbsp;
legendary weapon in his fight against Bo-Katan.

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:40.800
"The Darksaber is gone."

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:47.400
While Gideon's motivations are confusing,&nbsp;
the bigger question is how he even crushed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:51.300
the Darksaber with his hands. Not only does&nbsp;
he destroy it, but he does so with relative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:55.080
ease while somehow leaving Bo-Katan's&nbsp;
hand seemingly intact. It's not enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:58.500
to ruin the finale, but it's a glaring&nbsp;
plot hole that needs more explanation.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:01.980
"Star Wars" fans aren't exactly known&nbsp;
for their ability to let things go,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:06.360
so "The Mandalorian" getting rid of a powerful&nbsp;
"Star Wars" weapon like the Darksaber has proven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:10.920
to be a controversial move within the fandom. One&nbsp;
Reddit user commented after the episode aired,

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:13.080
"Kind of upset that the Darksaber got destroyed."

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:17.040
Other users had similarly strong opinions&nbsp;
on the fate of the legendary artifact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:21.000
with one fan calling it, quote, "the second&nbsp;
most emotional death of a non-character" in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.060
the greater "Star Wars" canon. While&nbsp;
it seems that most fans agree that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:27.720
the weapon's destruction was impactful,&nbsp;
the conversation quickly shifted to how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:30.600
its demise ultimately made sense&nbsp;
within the "Star Wars" universe.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:31.560
One user asked,

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:33.720
"How was Gideon able to&nbsp;
squash the hilt like that?"

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:35.160
Another viewer replied,

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:37.680
"I think his suit may have had&nbsp;
some mechanical improvements."

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:40.920
But another fan pointed out that the&nbsp;
beskar hilt should've withstood the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:44.640
hydraulic-assisted fist. One fan also&nbsp;
pointed out that Moff Gideon should have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:48.420
completely destroyed Bo-Katan's hand if he&nbsp;
had enough strength to crush the Darksaber.

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:52.140
Other users took a different approach,&nbsp;
suggesting that the death of the Darksaber&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:55.860
was for the best. One Reddit user admitted&nbsp;
they were happy to see it go, saying,

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:58.860
"Now the Mandos will just have to actually&nbsp;
pick the best leader in the future."

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:02.700
Another suggested that it plays into&nbsp;
Bo-Katan's character development, writing,

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:06.720
"[She] needed to realize she is still the&nbsp;
ruler of Mandalore even without the Darksaber."

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:10.200
Fans certainly have opinions on how the&nbsp;
Season 3 finale of "The Mandalorian"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:13.500
dealt with the Darksaber, but there's&nbsp;
a slight chance that the weapon isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:17.520
gone for good. From what fans know about&nbsp;
how lightsabers work, all variants of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:21.900
weapon are powered by a core kyber crystal. If&nbsp;
Gideon didn't crush the kyber crystal that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:26.160
Darksaber presumably contains within, then&nbsp;
its most vital component is still intact.

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:29.700
Some fans are even pretty confident that&nbsp;
the Darksaber will see a comeback in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:32.640
Season 4 of "The Mandalorian" or&nbsp;
beyond. One fan wrote on Reddit,

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:34.800
"There's definitely a good&nbsp;
chance it will get remade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:37.560
yeah. The kyber crystal inside&nbsp;
is probably still fine."

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:41.580
Some even took the theory a step further,&nbsp;
with one fan suggesting it will be none&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:45.780
other than the newly-anointed Din Grogu&nbsp;
who reforges the Darksaber. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:49.020
the fresh Mandalorian apprentice&nbsp;
needs a fittingly powerful weapon.

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:53.400
Whatever the ultimate fate of the Darksaber is,&nbsp;
its ostensible destruction in "The Mandalorian's"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:57.360
Season 3 finale will certainly have ripple&nbsp;
effects. It seems, at least for a time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:00.540
that the Mandalorians will have to learn&nbsp;
to live without the ancient weapon.

